    "created_at": "2025-10-22T22:48:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-22T22:48:12Z",
    "body": "Notes:\n\n- **Meeting Time Slot Review** Chris Wilson noted that the North America/APAC time slot, the current meeting time, had slightly less support in the poll than other options. They proposed cutting this time slot to have fewer meetings, given that it is difficult for many Europeans and was originally created primarily for Dominic, who sideshowbarker mentioned may no longer be regularly calling in. Chris Wilson plans to propose this change in Matrix and mentioned discussing it with Anne\n- **WhatWG Meeting at TPAC Reminder** Chris Wilson reminded attendees about the WhatWG meeting at TPAC, encouraging them to add any issues requiring more than a few minutes for resolution to the associated issue list for discussion. Anna specifically requested time for introspection regarding overall coordination and effectiveness, which Chris Wilson found to be a good idea for an open session on improving processes \n- **Postponed Topics for Next Week** Several agenda items were carried over to the following week due to the absence of key stakeholders like Anne, Mason, and other Mozilla representatives. These postponed topics included the XSLT issue, which was supposed to be revisited, and the proposal for form control range to advance to stage two, which Stephanie Zhang needed feedback on from Anna and people from Mozilla, specifically Keith and Mason.  Kurt Catti-Schmidt also decided to wait until the following week to discuss the declarative CSS modules work, particularly a new path suggested by Anne involving blobs instead of data \n- **Customized Built-in Elements via Element Internals Type** Alex Keng presented an updated explainer for customized built-in elements, seeking feedback on the approach of exposing only smaller building blocks like `command` and `command 4` The team investigated form association and ARIA behaviors as requested previously and decided to drop form association as unnecessary, but keep ARIA behaviors (focusability and role) to ensure accessibility by default, which was supported by the ARIA working group.  Alex Keng confirmed that Apple generally supports the idea but is waiting for a more concrete proposal, which the current explainer provides. Chris Wilson suggested carrying this item over to the next week to get input from more people\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
